    {
      "object_id": "mathia_synthesis_269c2dfe3afbe5fcab665ade99bde7fda5c21249cf542994f566ef4542db5fe8",
      "object_role": "synthesis",
      "proposed_content": "The source uses two complementary invariants to control different parts of the zero geometry: under RH, a positive real-part estimate for a reflected logarithmic derivative excludes derivative zeros on the critical-strip side, while boundary domination on far-left rectangles transfers a model zero count by Rouché and conjugation symmetry places each unique zero on the real axis. Together these arguments reduce the remaining uncertainty to bounded regions rather than requiring one estimate to work globally.",
